  "hero.kicker": "Computer science · Systems · an essay you can play with",
  "hero.title": "How a CPU Guesses Ahead",
  "hero.sub": "A branch takes a dozen cycles to say where it goes, and the CPU does not wait:<br>it guesses, gains when right, and starts over when wrong.",
  "hero.lead": "So there are three questions. How does it guess so well? Why can one CPU keep hundreds of instructions “in flight”? And why does this guessing leak secrets? We start with what a wrong guess costs, then build the predictors (remember the last outcome, look at the neighbours, look at histories of several lengths), the target buffer and the return stack. Then the core issues several instructions a cycle, runs them out of order, and commits them in order. Last, we see how large the window can grow, and how traces left on a wrong path can be read back. <strong>Every predictor is an algorithm from the literature, run branch by branch on a sequence the page generates; the core is a small simulator that runs cycle by cycle, so you can step through it.</strong>",
  "hero.note": "An AI wrote this page. Figures for real processors come from vendor documentation (AMD’s Zen 5 optimisation guide) and public measurements (Chips and Cheese), each dated where it appears. <strong>Where a value is marked “tuned parameter”, it was chosen so the model can compute, and the text says how the conclusions change if you alter it.</strong> The chapter on Spectre uses only a toy machine on the page. <strong>It contains no attack code that runs on a real computer.</strong> This page explains mechanisms only, and does not rate which chip is worth buying.",

  "toc.ch1": "Why a wrong guess costs more",
