  "ch1.title": "Why a wrong guess costs more",
  "ch1.law": "The deeper the pipeline and the more the core issues per cycle, the more work a wrong guess throws away. Misprediction cost = how often branches occur × how often they are wrong × cycles lost each time. That term adds directly to the average cycles each instruction takes.",
  "ch1.p1": "You reach a fork in the road, and the satnav is still calculating. You do not stop in the middle of the road to wait for it; you pick a lane and keep driving. If you pick right, you lose nothing. If you pick wrong, you have to turn back. <a href='../how-a-cpu-runs-a-program/#ch8'>Chapter 8 of “How a CPU Runs a Program”</a> covered this on a five-stage pipeline: a branch only learns whether it is taken in the third stage. Guessing “not taken” and being wrong flushes the two instructions already fetched behind it, and costs {a1Pen} cycles. This essay picks up from there.",
  "ch1.p2": "Today’s core is not five stages, and it does not issue one instruction per cycle. AMD’s Zen 5 optimisation guide says that a cycle sends at most {zW} instructions (macro-ops) to execution, and that the branch misprediction penalty is between {zPenMin} and {zPenMax} cycles, most often {zPen}. So one wrong guess wastes at most {zPen} × {zW} = {zSlots} instruction slots. Put that into the performance equation from <a href='../how-a-cpu-runs-a-program/#ch9'>chapter 9 of that essay</a>: cycles per instruction CPI = 1/width + branch fraction × misprediction rate × penalty. The branch fraction here is one branch per {fbrN} instructions. This is a <strong>tuned parameter</strong>, chosen only to show the right order of magnitude.",
  "ch1.model.title": "Cost of one wrong guess: the same accuracy on different cores",
  "ch1.model.how": "Drag width, penalty and accuracy to see how much of the time goes on wrong guesses. The two buttons set the core to the five-stage pipeline from that essay (width 1, penalty {a1Pen} cycles) or to the Zen 5 range (width {zW}, penalty {zPen} cycles). On the chart, the horizontal axis is accuracy. The gold curve is the core you set, and the grey curve is the five-stage pipeline.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Line chart: the horizontal axis is prediction accuracy and the vertical axis is the share of time lost to wrong guesses; two curves, one for the current core and one for the five-stage pipeline",
  "ch1.ctl.w": "Instructions issued per cycle",
  "ch1.ctl.p": "Misprediction penalty",
  "ch1.ctl.a": "Accuracy",
