  "ch1.btn.zen": "Zen 5 range",
  "ch1.read.cpi": "Average cycles per instruction (CPI)",
  "ch1.read.ipc": "Instructions completed per cycle (IPC)",
  "ch1.read.lost": "Time spent on wrong guesses",
  "ch1.read.slots": "Instruction slots wasted by one wrong guess",
  "ch1.cv.x": "Accuracy",
  "ch1.cv.y": "Time lost to wrong guesses",
  "ch1.cv.you": "Current core",
  "ch1.cv.a1": "Five-stage pipeline",
  "ch1.say.cheap": "At accuracy {acc}, wrong guesses take only {lost} of the time. This core is narrow and the penalty is small, so a wrong guess does not hurt much.",
  "ch1.say.mid": "At accuracy {acc}, {lost} of the time goes on wrong guesses, and the core completes only {ipc} instructions a cycle, short of the ideal {w}.",
  "ch1.say.dear": "At accuracy {acc}, <em>more than half the time ({lost}) goes on wrong guesses</em>: the core completes only {ipc} instructions a cycle. A wide, deep core needs accuracy above 99% before it pays.",
  "ch1.p3": "At the same 95% accuracy, the five-stage pipeline loses only {a1Lost} of its time. On a core in the Zen 5 range, the loss is {z95Lost}, and the core completes only {z95Ipc} instructions a cycle instead of {zW}. At 90% accuracy the loss is {z90Lost}; at 99% it is still {z99Lost}, at {z99Ipc} instructions a cycle. Put another way, 99% means one wrong guess per {per99} instructions on average, and 95% means one per {per95}. <strong>The wider and deeper the core, the more accurate the predictor must be.</strong> That is why modern processors spend so many transistors on prediction.",
  "ch1.limit": "The branch fraction in the model is a tuned parameter. At one branch per {fbr10} instructions, the loss at 95% accuracy is {zf1Lost}; at one per {fbr3} it is {zf3Lost}. The direction of the conclusion does not change. “1/width” is the ideal value, and real programs do not reach it, because of data dependences and cache misses (chapters 6 and 9). For what the pipeline does while memory stalls, see <a href='../why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch1'>chapter 1 of “Why Compute Waits for Memory”</a>.",

  "ch2.title": "Remember the last time: 1-bit and 2-bit counters",
  "ch2.law": "Most branches have their own habits. A loop’s closing branch, which jumps back, almost always jumps; a check for an error almost never does. Store two bits for each branch, remembering its recent habit, and you guess right most of the time.",
  "ch2.p1": "The simplest way to choose a route to work is “whatever I took yesterday, I take today”. The earliest dynamic branch predictors did exactly that. They used a few low bits of the branch’s address to index a table, and the table recorded whether that branch was taken last time (a <strong>1-bit predictor</strong>). There are also <strong>static</strong> rules that consult no table: always guess not taken (chapter 8 of that essay), or “backward branches are taken, forward branches are not”, because a backward branch is usually the bottom of a loop.",
  "ch2.p2": "The 1-bit predictor has a flaw at loop exits. On the last pass the branch is not taken, and that is recorded as “not taken”. The next time the loop starts, its first pass is mispredicted, so each exit is wrong twice. In 1981 James Smith proposed the change to a <strong>2-bit saturating counter</strong>: four states, 0 to 3. States 2 and 3 predict taken. A taken outcome adds one, a not-taken outcome subtracts one, and the counter stops at the ends. It takes two wrong outcomes in a row to change its mind, so an occasional exception does not lead it astray.",
  "dg.fsm.aria": "State diagram of a 2-bit saturating counter: four states; a taken outcome (T) moves towards state 3, a not-taken outcome (NT) towards state 0; states 2 and 3 predict taken",
  "dg.fsm.cap": "A 2-bit saturating counter. Each branch has one of these in the table. Arrows marked T are taken outcomes and move one step towards state 3; arrows marked NT are not-taken outcomes and move one step towards state 0; the counter stops at the ends. States 3 and 2 predict “taken”, states 1 and 0 predict “not taken”. Schematic.",
  "dg.fsm.s0": "Strongly not taken",
  "dg.fsm.s1": "Weakly not taken",
  "dg.fsm.s2": "Weakly taken",
  "dg.fsm.s3": "Strongly taken",
  "dg.fsm.t": "T",
  "dg.fsm.n": "NT",
  "dg.fsm.pn": "Predict: not taken",
  "dg.fsm.pt": "Predict: taken",
  "ch2.model.title": "Four ways to guess, one loop",
  "ch2.model.how": "A two-level loop: the inner loop runs K times, and inside it an if skips a block of code with probability p. Drag K and p to see how many each of the four guessing methods gets right. Choose one method and the grid below draws its guesses for the first {strip} branches (green is right, red is wrong; the number in each cell is the 2-bit counter’s state before the guess).",
  "ch2.model.aria": "Grid: a predictor’s guesses for the first few dozen branches, green for right and red for wrong",
  "ch2.ctl.k": "Inner loop count K",
  "ch2.ctl.p": "Probability p that the if is taken",
  "ch2.ctl.kind": "Which method to draw",
  "ch2.kind.nt": "Always not taken",
  "ch2.kind.btfn": "Backward taken",
